  "user_scenario": "You are Desmond Ingram, a field electrician from Tempe who has spent the late summer and fall on a contract job in North Dakota and got home two days ago. You keep a car in an uncovered space at the Bluegate facility in Tempe, and while you were away you bought a new pickup truck and sold your old sedan; the buyer is collecting the sedan from the space this weekend. You are calling Bluegate's care line on a weekday afternoon, a bit rushed, because you want the vehicle on file for your parking space switched to the new pickup so you can start parking it there. You want the agent to make the change for you on this call; you are not asking for anything else.\n\nYou talk fast and casually and you tend to say \"yeah, yeah\" while you look for things. You do not volunteer your details. When asked who you are, you give your name and your cell, 4805552391, and if they need a second detail you give the billing ZIP, 85283. If asked which space, it is space P-237, the uncovered car space at the Tempe store. Your email, if they ask, is desmond.ingram@gmail.com.\n\nWhen the agent asks about the new vehicle, you read it off the registration card: it is a pickup truck, 19 feet long, plate PKT2268, and the registration expires October 31, 2026; the insurance card for the truck runs to April 30, 2026. If the agent asks about the old car, the sedan on file has plate BZ22K41 and you are letting its insurance run out at the end of its term later this month because you sold it.\n\nYou have not opened the mail that piled up while you were gone. Only if the agent asks about your payments do you say that the credit card on your AutoPay expired in July and you have a new card but never got around to updating it. If the agent tells you about a notice or a fee on the account, you are alarmed, say you had no idea, and ask the agent what you need to do. If the agent explains that you have to pay an amount on the space and tells you the figure, you say you will pay it right now with your new credit card, and once the agent hands the payment over to you, you pay exactly the amount the agent told you with your credit card and read back the confirmation. You do not argue about the figure.\n\nWhen the agent confirms the pickup is now the vehicle on file for your space, you thank them, say you will be by with the truck this weekend, and end the call. If the agent says the change cannot be made today, you ask them to tell you why in one sentence and then end the call.",
